# Apache rules for the end-to-end container, and the reason they are a file in
# this repository instead of something WordPress writes for itself.
#
# WordPress writes these rules into `.htaccess` when pretty permalinks are saved.
# In the containerised environment it CANNOT: the document root belongs to root
# inside the image, while Apache and WP-CLI both run as the host user, so every
# attempt to create the file fails - silently, because saving a permalink
# structure that cannot be written still stores the structure in the database.
#
# The result measured on the first run of this harness: WordPress reported
# `/%postname%/` permalinks and Apache answered 404 to every path that was not a
# real file. That takes the whole ingestion path with it - Apache never hands
# `/api/webhooks/stores/woocommerce` to PHP, so the mock receiver never sees a
# delivery and every delivery scenario fails with a transport error that looks
# exactly like a plugin defect.
#
# So the rules are shipped and mounted. They are WordPress's own, unmodified.
#
# NOTHING HERE IS PUBLISHED: `.distignore` excludes the whole `tests/` tree, and
# the plugin has no opinion about a merchant's Apache configuration.

# BEGIN WordPress
<IfModule mod_rewrite.c>
RewriteEngine On
RewriteRule .* - [E=HTTP_AUTHORIZATION:%{HTTP:Authorization}]
RewriteBase /
RewriteRule ^index\.php$ - [L]
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-f
RewriteCond %{REQUEST_FILENAME} !-d
RewriteRule . /index.php [L]
</IfModule>
# END WordPress
